\title{P7: where the wall clock of 8 workers goes}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{driver}
\tag{gc}
\tag{platform}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/P7.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Agent "P7" (parallel scaling), 2026-11-24, 15:28Z to 18:35Z. Question: the machine engine checked all of Mathlib with 8 workers in 80 s of wall clock for 278 s of CPU on the loaded host, a speed-up of about 3.4, and 4 workers were no slower. Where does the wall clock of a parallel run go, what of it is serial, and what can the driver (\code{\verb~|rlvm-driver~}, which this agent owns since 15:33Z) do about it?}

\p{Short answer. On quiet cores the 3.4 was the host: the check phase scales 6.9x from 8 workers on Mathlib (7.4x in a check-only pass), 6.9x on Std, 6.3x on cedar. What keeps a whole pipelined run at 5.5x - 5.9x is, in this order: (1) \strong{collections}: every pause of the single-threaded collector stops all 8 workers and the loader: 3.9 s of a 37.7 s pipelined run (10 \%), 1.1 s of a 30.6 s check phase (3.5 \%); the garbage is session arrays of the machine and of the store's overlay that are grown again after each of 14,000 session resets per pass, plus the pipelined loader's; (2) \strong{10 runnable threads on 8 cores} while the file loads (loader 12 s + environment thread 3 - 5 s of CPU are part of the job; on 8 cores they are 2 s of wall clock); (3) \strong{the worker CPU itself grows} by 6 \% from 2 to 8 workers, which is the hardware's (eight one-worker processes over eight shards use the same CPU: section 5); (4) on the mid-size corpora \strong{one heavy unit} (a declined declaration re-checked by the reference kernel) decides when the run ends. Nothing is lost to locks, to the frontier or to the tail on Mathlib (parks 0.4 \% - 1.1 \%).}

\p{What the driver could do about it, it now does: its own waits were 1 \% on Mathlib, so the scheduler changes move only the mid-size corpora (Std check-only passes with 8 workers 2.15 -> 1.65 s), but the pipelined hand-off did cost the workers something: every publication makes each worker context copy the level tables, and publishing a 6 GB file in 52 pieces instead of 205 takes Mathlib with 8 workers from 34.6 s to 31.8 s (-8 \%, two interleaved rounds). Curve now, pipelined: 208.3 s (1 worker), 104.9 (2), 53.8 (4), 31.8 (8): 6.5x from 8 workers (7.0x against a one-worker batch run), where the machine's ceiling for eight cores is 7.6x - 7.9x.}

\p{Method: section 1. Numbers: sections 2 - 5. What was changed in the driver and what it bought: section 6. What was handed to other owners: section 7. The three next things: section 8.}

\subtree{
\title{1. Method}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Quiet cores.} Every wall-clock number went through \src{bench/quiet.py} (the 8 performance cores 5-9,15-17 reserved, one measurement at a time; between 16:02Z and 17:12Z through a private copy with a one-line parsing fix, same lock). The host's load average was 45 - 140 throughout; what leaks onto the measurement cores between two herding rounds shows as run-queue time of about 5 \% in the 8-worker rows (the driver measures it, so it is known rather than hidden). Single runs unless a table says otherwise; differences below 3 \% are not results.}
\li{\strong{Binary.} CE native image \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine -X machine.switches=warm,gated,expect~}, session cap 4 MiB. Private distributions that differ only in the driver jar (\code{\verb~|work/p7/tools/relink.sh~}: the jars of \code{\verb~|dist-a~} + one driver jar, the image's flags as \src{scripts/build.sh} sets them): \code{\verb~|dist-a~} = the published jars of 15:31Z (driver c0aeb3288f46, machine 904d8ff41d25, store f85c730603b8, load 26242def2701, admit 143aff8926eb); \code{\verb~|dist-b~} = + the time account (no behaviour change); \code{\verb~|dist-c~} = + a split bound, the kept pass, the parking rule; \code{\verb~|dist-d~} = + the shared-bytes fix; \code{\verb~|dist-e~} = + slices, the parking rule on all roots (published as 150aed4bf1bf with kept engines on top); \code{\verb~|dist-f~} = + the publication scale (published as 31fa08d9ce61). JVM launcher rows: the launcher of \code{\verb~|dist-d~} with its default flags (13 GB heap, \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~}, serial).}
\li{\strong{The driver's time account} (new; \src{rlvm-driver/README.md}, "Where the wall clock goes"): per worker, wall = on a CPU + runnable without a CPU (both from \code{\verb~|/proc/thread-self/schedstat~}) + parked in the scheduler by cause (nothing announced by the environment lane / the lane waits for the loader / tail: only busy claims left) + heavy gate + waiting for the end of the load + the rest (stopped by a collection, a page fault); the environment thread's account; time stamps; time series; slowest units; a per-unit trace. \src{rlvm-driver/bin/breakdown.py} prints it. Collections: \code{\verb~|-XX:+PrintGC~} of the image (\code{\verb~|gcsum.py~}), \code{\verb~|-Xlog:gc,safepoint~} on the JVM; allocation sites: JFR on the JVM launcher (every allocation outside a TLAB and every new TLAB).}
\li{\strong{Warm and check-only}: \code{\verb~|RLVM_DRIVER=passes=N~} (the driver's passes: 2..N check the loaded store again).}
\li{\strong{Hardware ceiling}: \code{\verb~|-X shard=K/N~} (new, measurement only): 8 one-worker processes over the 8 shards of the units, each pinned to one core, against one process with 8 workers.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Mathlib, native image (721,845 units, 6.33 GB)}

\p{Wall clock in seconds (dist-b, 16:10Z - 16:36Z):}

