\p{(dist-b, 16:03Z - 16:07Z; the baseline before the instrumentation, dist-a at 15:45Z, is the same within 3 \%.)}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Std with 8 workers is bounded by one unit.} Unit 23249 is the largest proof of the file (76,204 block nodes), is declined by the machine and re-checked by the reference kernel: 0.7 s on a quiet core, 1.09 s when ten threads share eight cores. 8 workers have 1.5 s of CPU each, and the unit sits at about half of the work. Share of the tail: 5.8 \% (batch), 17.6 \% (pipelined), and 27.7 \% in a check-only pass of the old scheduler, which cut the whole file into 8 contiguous eighths: the unit sat near the end of its eighth and started at 1.4 s of a run that could have ended at 1.5 s.}
\li{Units the warm machine does not accept: Std 203 units = 9.7 \% of the time in units; Mathlib 6,711 units (0.94 \%) = 10.7 \% (per-unit traces; time shares from runs on the loaded host, counts exact).}
\li{Size predicts cost: on Std the two slowest units are the two largest, 9 of the 12 slowest are among the 40 largest (of 100,096), Spearman rank correlation of size and machine steps 0.83.}
\li{Pipelined against batch on the small files: the load (1.1 s / 1.6 s alone, 1.3 s / 2.4 s next to 8 workers) is the length of the run's first part; the gain is the load's CPU time moved onto idle cores (2 - 4 workers: all of it; 8 workers: a third).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The hardware ceiling}

\p{\code{\verb~|-X shard=K/8~} makes a one-worker process check only shard K of the units (blocks of 1,024 units, block number modulo 8) after a complete load and environment pass; passes 2 and 3 are check-only. Eight such processes, each pinned to one of the eight measurement cores (\code{\verb~|work/p7/tools/ceiling.sh mathlib~}, dist-e, 17:35Z), against one process with eight workers:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{check-only pass, wall}\th{CPU}}
\tr{\td{8 one-worker processes at once, per shard}\td{23.7 / 24.2 / 25.1 / 25.2 / 26.7 / 28.7 / 29.3 / 31.2 s (mean 26.8 s; third pass mean 26.6 s)}\td{sum 213.0 s (third pass 210.7 s)}}
\tr{\td{the same shard alone on its core (shards 0 and 4)}\td{23.89 s and 22.21 s (at once: 25.14 s and 23.71 s)}\td{at once +5.2 \% and +6.7 \%}}
\tr{\td{one process, 8 workers}\td{27.9 - 28.7 s}\td{workers 209.5 - 212.7 s}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{One process costs no more CPU than eight}: 210 - 213 s either way. Nothing that the workers share (store pages, the fact store, the scheduler, the verdict board) shows up as CPU.}
\li{\strong{Eight busy cores make each of them 5 \% - 7 \% slower} (memory bandwidth and the shared cache; the store is 2.1 GB of mostly random reads): this is the growth of the worker CPU from 2 to 8 workers (201 -> 213 s) and it is the hardware's. So the ceiling of this machine for a check-only pass over Mathlib is about 26.6 s (the mean shard time at once = a perfectly balanced 8-way split), i.e. 7.6 - 7.9 times one worker.}
\li{\strong{One process with 8 workers is 5 \% - 8 \% above that ceiling} (27.9 - 28.7 s): the collections (1.0 - 1.5 s of stop-the-world, which eight separate heaps do not share) and the host's noise. A static split is worse than the scheduler: the shards differ by 30 \% (23.7 s to 31.2 s), so eight independent processes end after 31.2 s.}
\li{Std and cedar (the same experiment, 18:20Z): Std shards 1.13 - 2.21 s, mean 1.48 s (sum 11.7 - 12.0 s), one process 1.63 - 1.67 s; cedar 1.66 - 2.77 s, mean 2.02 s (sum 16.1 - 16.3 s), one process 2.15 - 2.20 s; a shard alone on its core is 4.5 \% - 5.1 \% faster on both. Same picture: no CPU lost to sharing a process; 7 \% - 13 \% above the ceiling, on Std because of the one heavy unit.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What was changed in the driver}

\p{All in \code{\verb~|rlvm-driver~} (jars 150aed4bf1bf, published 18:03:49Z, and 31fa08d9ce61, published 18:27:41Z: + the publication scale); tests 107 (104 run green, 3 opt-in); guards at the end of this section. Default behaviour and every verdict are unchanged; what changed is who checks a unit and when, what the statistics say, and what a pipelined run decides for files that are not in declaration order (now what the batch run decides).}

\table{
\tr{\th{Change}\th{Why}\th{Measured}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Slices} (\code{\verb~|RunConfig.sliceUnits~} = 256, \code{\verb~|-X slice=N~}; 0 = the former scheme): a worker without work takes the next slice of the ready units for itself; nothing is put into another worker's queue}\td{with one contiguous run per worker and batch, a worker that fell behind kept receiving its share of every later batch and nobody else touched it while anything was left to split: a heavy unit in that backlog started late; a later pass cut the whole file into W eighths}\td{Std, check-only passes: 8 workers 2.15 - 2.18 s -> 1.67 s (median of 20; -23 \%), 4 workers 3.37 - 3.53 -> 2.92 - 3.00 s. Pass 1 and pipelined on Std (ten rounds against a split that is merely bounded): medians 1.67 / 1.68 s and 2.03 / 1.99 s, i.e. equal; three of nineteen pass-1 samples of the former scheme at 2.2 - 2.4 s, none of twelve with slices (too few to call). cedar, Mathlib: no difference (Mathlib, 8 workers, one window: 41.4 / 28.4 / 34.7 s before, 41.5 / 27.9 / 35.9 s after for batch / check-only / pipelined; steals 286 -> 3)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{The environment thread adds up the store's bytes at most every 20 ms} (was: after every step)}\td{\code{\verb~|Store.sharedBytes()~} walks one slot per unit: 4.0 of the thread's 4.85 s on Mathlib}\td{Mathlib, batch, 8 workers: environment thread 4.69 s CPU / 5.13 s wall -> 1.56 s / 1.67 s, the frontier is complete 3.5 s earlier, parks at the frontier 0.7 \% -> 0.0 \%; in wall clock inside the noise (0.4 s of 29 s expected). Std, cedar: nothing (small tables)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{The environment pass is kept over the end of a pipelined load; parking rule} (\code{\verb~|RunHost.unitNeedsCompleteFile~})}\td{a replaced pass had lost the claims of blocks it had decided ahead; the pass moved past units whose proof (or, in a race, type) mentions a later declaration}\td{invariance suite: pipelined declines 5 -> 0 of 16,928 mutants with the machine, 0 with the reference engine; 0 parked units on Init, Std, Mathlib}}
\tr{\td{\strong{A large file is published in larger pieces} (\code{\verb~|RunConfig.publishScaleMax~} = 4, \code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=N~}): the pipelined hand-off interval times the input's size in GiB, at most 4; unchanged below 2 GiB}\td{every publication makes each worker context copy the level tables at its next reset: about 110 MB of garbage and 45 ms of worker CPU per publication on Mathlib, and the collections stop all 8 workers}\td{\strong{Mathlib pipelined, 8 workers, two interleaved rounds: 34.25 / 35.00 s -> 31.71 / 31.90 s (-8 \%)}; 4 workers 55.71 -> 53.80 s (-3 \%); 205 -> 52 publications, garbage 36.9 -> 20.9 GB, pauses 3.75 -> 2.23 s, worker CPU 221.3 -> 214.8 s, peak RSS 4.1 -> 3.8 GB; Std, cedar: the same interval, the same time}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Time account, per-unit trace, statistics of every later pass, \code{\verb~|shard=K/N~}}; per-unit statistics per worker instead of one lock per unit}\td{this report}\td{off without a statistics file}}
\tr{\td{\strong{\code{\verb~|-X passes-keep=engines~}}: the later passes use the engine instances of pass 1}\td{asked for by the Truffle tier and the code store (requests.log 16:31Z, 16:57Z)}\td{3 passes over one set of engines through the launcher}}
}

\p{Guards (details in \src{rlvm-driver/README.md}, "Measured", wave 8): \src{scripts/gate.sh} on a private distribution, batch with one worker and pipelined with four (small files forced through the pipeline): 230 / 230 published tests, tutorial mutants 12,260 with SOUNDNESS 0 / COMPLETENESS 0 / agree 11,847 / declined 413 in both modes, differential self-test caught; slice + self-test again on the published jar; the driver's invariance suite (every file in 9 configurations: 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 workers, 1-byte session caps, pipelined with a publication after every 1 / 7 / 64 units): published tests 231 / 231, mutants 16,928 with the reference engine and with the machine, the four big corpora with the machine: 0 wrong, 0 schedule differences, 0 pipelined declines (5 before); Mathlib accepted by every build.}

\p{Not done, with the measurement that says why: \strong{stealing from the tail of a busy claim} (tail 0.0 \% - 0.4 \% of 8 workers x wall on Mathlib; on Std the tail is one unit, not a claim); \strong{the environment lane's work on the workers} (1.6 s - 2.3 s of CPU on Mathlib, parks at the frontier 0.0 \% - 0.7 \%); \strong{wake-ups and lock hold times} (time inside \code{\verb~|Scheduler.claim~} that is not a park: below 0.05 s per worker in a 30 s run).}
}

