\subtree{
\title{8. The next three things for the 8-worker wall clock}

\p{In the order of what they would bring on Mathlib with 8 workers (the driver's own waits being 1 \%):}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Do not make the garbage} (P7-F1; machine and store owners): a session reset that keeps its arrays, a worker context that does not copy the level tables at every snapshot, a re-check lane that keeps its overlay. The collector is the one serial component whose share grows with the worker count: 1.1 s of a 28 - 30 s check phase and, after the publication scale, still 2.2 s of a 31.8 s pipelined run; it is the 5 \% - 8 \% that separate one process from the hardware ceiling. A larger young generation does not do it (26 collections of 123 ms instead of 403 of 10 ms: each one copies the live session arrays).}
\li{\strong{The units the machine declines} (P7-F3; machine owners): 0.94 \% of Mathlib's units take 10.7 \% of the time inside units (measured on the loaded host, so indicative) at reference-kernel speed: about 20 s of the 213 s of worker CPU, up to 2.5 s of wall clock with 8 workers; on Std one such unit is the end of the run. In the driver, the companion is to start large units first (the size predicts the cost; \src{rlvm-driver/README.md}, "Open", has the progress rule such a scheduler must keep): Std with 8 workers pipelined about 2.0 -> 1.7 s, Mathlib nothing.}
\li{\strong{The warm JVM} (the platform question of owner note 12, for the steward): a check-only pass over Mathlib with 8 workers takes 23.6 s on the JVM launcher against 28.4 s in the native image (worker CPU 174 s against 210 s: C2's code), for 6 - 11 GB of resident set against 3.6 - 4.0 GB; cold, the two are equal (42.6 s batch, 34.6 s pipelined). Where steady state counts, this is 17 \% at once, more than anything the scheduler can still give.}
}

\p{Not on the list because it is not serial but simply work: loader (12 s) and environment thread (1.6 - 3 s) are 15 s of CPU, 1.9 s of wall clock on 8 cores, and with 8 workers on 8 cores they are why a pipelined run has ten runnable threads for 20 s. On a machine with ten cores the pipelined run would be the check-only time.}
}
